# Task 2: Ubuntu Server + Windows 11 guests on my laptop

**Host (Task 1):** ASUS TUF F15 FX506HE, Windows 11 Pro, i5-11400H (6 cores / 12 threads, virtualization on), 8 GB RAM (about 7.9 GB usable, only ~0.7 GB free in Task 1), 512 GB NVMe (about 476 GiB). Hypervisor: VirtualBox 7, which takes the guests' RAM, CPU and disk from the host.

## Verdict
This computer cannot run both guests at their official minimums and still leave the host usable. The limit is RAM. The closest working configuration runs Windows 11 with 3 GB, below its 4 GB minimum.

## 1. Allocation

| | Ubuntu Server 24.04 | Windows 11 Pro |
|---|---|---|
| vCPUs | 1 | 2 |
| Memory | 1024 MB | 3072 MB (below the 4 GB minimum) |
| Disk | 25 GB, dynamic | 64 GB, dynamic |
| Network | Adapter 1: NAT. Adapter 2: Host-only | Adapter 1: NAT |

## 2. Arithmetic

**Assumed host needs:** 3.0 GB RAM (idle Windows 11 plus VirtualBox), 4 threads, about 120 GB disk already used (my assumption, replace with the Task 1 figure) and 20% of the SSD kept free. Hypervisor overhead is about 0.2 GB per guest.

| Case | VMs + overhead | Left for host (7.9 GB) | Enough (3.0 GB needed)? |
|---|---|---|---|
| A. Both at official minimums (4 + 1 GB) | 5.4 GB | 2.5 GB | No, short by 0.5 GB. Also below the 4 GB Windows 11 itself needs |
| B. My plan (3 + 1 GB) | 4.4 GB | 3.5 GB | Yes, margin 0.5 GB |

- **CPU:** 3 vCPUs out of 12 threads, so 9 threads are left. This is not a constraint.
- **Disk:** worst case both disks fill, 25 + 64 = 89 GB. 476 − 120 − 89 = about 267 GB free, well above the 95 GB reserve.

## 3. Justification
- **Ubuntu 1 vCPU / 1 GB / 25 GB:** it only runs sshd, so the minimum is enough. 2 GB would eat the host's margin.
- **Windows 2 vCPUs:** this is the Windows 11 minimum, and more would add pressure for no gain.
- **Windows 3 GB, below the minimum:** at 4 GB the host is left with 2.5 GB and starts paging heavily. At 2 GB Windows would be too slow. 3 GB is the compromise. Setup refuses less than 4 GB, so I bypass only the RAM check (`BypassRAMCheck` in `HKLM\SYSTEM\Setup\LabConfig`). TPM 2.0 and Secure Boot stay genuine, because VirtualBox 7 provides both. It is unsupported by Microsoft, so it is fine for a lab but not for production.
- **Dynamic disks:** fixed disks would commit 89 GB at once for almost no speed gain on NVMe, and there is plenty of free space.
- **Network, Ubuntu NAT + Host-only:** NAT gives internet and hides the guest. Host-only gives the host a private address to ssh to, with no port forwarding.
  - NAT alone needs a port-forward rule.
  - Bridged over laptop Wi-Fi is unreliable and exposes sshd to the office LAN.
  - Host-only alone has no internet.
- **Windows NAT only:** it needs internet and nobody needs to reach it.

## 4. Verification plan (both guests running)

| Where | Command | Expected output |
|---|---|---|
| Host | `VBoxManage list runningvms` | Two VMs listed |
| Host | `VBoxManage showvminfo <vm> --machinereadable \| findstr /i "^memory= ^cpus= ^nic"` | Ubuntu: `memory=1024`, `cpus=1`, `nic1="nat"`, `nic2="hostonly"`. Windows: `memory=3072`, `cpus=2`, `nic1="nat"` |
| Host | `Test-NetConnection 192.168.56.101 -Port 22` | `TcpTestSucceeded : True` |
| Host | `ssh <user>@192.168.56.101` | `Welcome to Ubuntu 24.04 LTS ...` |
| Host | `(Get-CimInstance Win32_OperatingSystem).FreePhysicalMemory` | About 500000 KB or more free |
| Ubuntu | `nproc` | `1` |
| Ubuntu | `free -m` | Total about 900 to 990 |
| Ubuntu | `lsblk -d -o NAME,SIZE,TYPE` | `sda 25G disk` |
| Ubuntu | `ip -br addr` | `enp0s3 UP 10.0.2.15/24` and `enp0s8 UP 192.168.56.101/24` |
| Ubuntu | `curl -sI https://ubuntu.com \| head -1` | `HTTP/2 200` or `301` |
| Ubuntu | `ping -c 3 8.8.8.8` | 0% packet loss |
| Windows | `(Get-CimInstance Win32_ComputerSystem).NumberOfLogicalProcessors` | `2` |
| Windows | `[math]::Round((Get-CimInstance Win32_ComputerSystem).TotalPhysicalMemory/1GB,1)` | About `3` |
| Windows | `Get-Disk` | One disk of 64 GB |
| Windows | `ipconfig` | IPv4 `10.0.2.15`, gateway `10.0.2.2` |
| Windows | `Test-NetConnection www.microsoft.com -Port 443` | `TcpTestSucceeded : True` |

The address `192.168.56.101` is an example, so use the one the Host-only adapter really gets.

## 5. What it gives up
- A slower, unsupported Windows guest.
- Only about 0.5 GB of host margin, so heavy host apps should stay closed.
- Not suitable for always-on use.
- The real fix is a second 8 GB RAM module (16 GB total), if the laptop has a free slot.
